    "searchText": "You are a consultant engaged by the board of directors (‘the board’) of Scottish National \nBank Ltd (‘SNB’). SNB is a private equity owned bank with two trading arms, investment \nbanking and retail banking. SNB is headquartered in Glasgow, operates UK-wide, and \nhas branches in several European cities.  \n  \nYou have held a meeting with Jenny McMillan (chief talent officer), to gather more \ninformation relevant to your engagement. Jenny explained that SNB’s payroll operations \nare organised in core groups which comprise the client-facing and back-office functions, \nand call centre team. In respect of this engagement, she is concerned about the payroll \nprocesses for the call centre payrolls as in recent months, there have been a number of \nerrors and delays. You have prepared payroll system process notes (Appendix 1) and \nJenny has asked you to identify the weaknesses, analyse the implications and \nrecommend improvements in the payroll process for the call centre payroll.  \n   \nRequired: \n  \nIdentify the weaknesses, analyse the implications and recommend improvements in the \npayroll process for the call centre payroll.  \n  \n(30 marks) \n \n \n APPENDIX 1 – Payroll System process notes from discussion with Jenny McMillan, \nchief talent officer \n  \nSNB has two payroll groups that are managed as follows: \n  \n• Client-facing and back-office functions – outsourced by each local location and not to \nbe considered in the current project \n• Call centre staff – all staff based in the UK and managed on an in-house payroll system, \nPayroll VIP (‘VIP’) \n  \nSNB’s payroll department comprises six members of staff, all of whom have access to \nVIP. The human resources (‘HR’) team is responsible for the engagement and termination \nof employees and for maintaining the employee masterfile. Due to high staff turnover \nwithin the call centres, members of the payroll department spend one or two days a month \nassisting HR in processing changes to the employee masterfile. \n  \nCall centre staff \nVIP is a standalone, in-house-developed payroll system that has been in operation for \nover 10 years. Upgrades and modifications have been carried out by the in-house IT \ndepartment.  \n  \nEach member of the payroll department has a unique alphanumeric log-in and password \nfor VIP that they are prompted to change on a regular basis. Amanda Li, head of payroll, \nis prompted each month, after the payroll run, to back-up VIP to the firm’s cloud storage \ndrive. The system log shows that this has been done each month, but the back-ups have \nnot been tested for 13 months.  \n  \nVIP is used to process payroll for circa 2,000 employees of SNB, who work in three UK \ncall centres and are all paid on an hourly basis. A standard shift is either eight or four \nhours depending on the employee’s schedule, and all staff are paid on a monthly basis \non the last working day of the month. Remuneration is calculated based on each \nemployee’s hourly rate for hours worked up to the 20th of that month. Call centre staff are \nall paid at a rate 20% greater than the UK Government’s minimum statutory hourly rate \nfor their age. Jenny noted that a number of staff had queried their rate of pay with the \npayroll department during the year as their rate of pay had not increased in line with their \nage. A number of employees’ wages were investigated and were found to have been \nunderpaid for several months. Once investigated, the shortfalls were processed through \nthe following month’s payroll.  \n  \n All joiners and leavers matters are dealt with by SNB’s HR team. When starters complete \ntheir ‘new starters’ form for HR, they have the option to waive their rights under UK \nnational legislation to work a maximum of 48 hours per week. Some employees take up \nthis option signing their ‘new starters’ form as evidence of this. \n  \nWhen an employee leaves, their supervisor completes a termination form and sends this \nto HR. HR informs Amanda of the final date for the employee and their accrued holiday \npay for Amanda to include in their final payroll payment.  \n   \nPayroll processing  \nAll call centre staff are paid based on time logged into the call centre customer support \ntelephone system (‘CST’). At the start of their shift, staff log into CST using their staff \nnumber and password. The system automatically logs all staff out after eight hours, \nregardless of their scheduled shift, unless they are actively on a call or if the call centre \nsupervisor overrides the automatic logout via their unique log-in authorisation code. This \nis usually due to an employee needing to go home due to illness or an emergency. \n  \nMonth end procedures: VIP \nOn the 23rd of the month, a payroll clerk runs an in-house developed program to upload \nthe CST hours into VIP for preparing the payroll run. VIP then automatically produces the \npayroll listing, payslips and BACS file. The payroll clerk manually generates and posts \nthe journal entries for the payroll related entries (salaries, income tax, pensions etc) to \nthe call centre payroll accounts in the general ledger. These are reviewed and approved \nby the payroll manager. \n  \nJenny reviews the payroll run each month but, due to time constraints, rarely raises any \nqueries if the total does not vary too much from the previous month’s total. This review is \nusually done after payment. \n  \nThe BACS file is encrypted and transmitted by the senior payroll clerk, following approval \nby Amanda or Jenny, to SNB’s BACS processing centre for payment. A print out of the \npayroll listing is stored securely in the payroll department until the end of the tax year, \nwhen it is moved into storage.  \n  \nA payroll clerk prepares monthly general ledger reconciliations for all the call centre \npayroll salaries, PAYE, NIC, pensions and other deductions. These reconciliations are \nreviewed and evidenced by Amanda and retained for future reference and in case they \nare requested for review purposes.  \n  \n Two exception reports are generated by the payroll clerk from VIP on a monthly basis \nand filed with the payroll listing. These show any employees working more than 48 hours \nper week and automatic logout overrides by shift supervisors. \n  \n  \n \n",
