    "searchText": "Scenario 2 - Purchase system \nYou are an audit senior working on the audit of Anglia Sweets Ltd (‘Anglia’). Anglia manufactures \ntraditional British sweets and confectionery in its factory in Norwich and distributes them \ndirectly to retailers and indirectly through national distributors.  \nYou have been provided with the following information and have been asked to identify and \nevaluate key controls and design tests of control to verify whether the controls are operating \neffectively. \nOrders \nEmployees within the various user departments of Anglia determine when they need to \npurchase any specific raw materials or require services. The employee will create a Purchase \nRequisition (‘PR’) which is then authorised by the user department manager by electronic \nsignature. \nThe buyer selects which supplier to fulfil the order. This is fully at their discretion as there is no \ncompany approved supplier list. The buyer raises a five-part sequentially-numbered purchase \norder (‘PO’) and sends one copy to the supplier, one to the accounts department, one to the \nuser department and retains a fourth for the purchasing department’s records. A report of all \nPOs is run on a weekly basis and reviewed and approved by the purchasing manager to confirm \nall PO numbers have been allocated in sequence and relate to a valid company-approved PR.   \nReceipt of goods \nAll goods are received in the warehouse where a trained member of staff signs for receipt of the \ngoods, after reviewing the quality of the goods, and raises a goods received note (‘GRN’). After \nreceipt, the GRN and proof of delivery note from the supplier are sent to the buyers to enable \nthem to be matched to the PO.  \nAll deliveries received in the warehouse are agreed to the PO prior to acceptance. Where there \nare any discrepancies, these are resolved prior to acceptance. Goods are receipted into the \ngeneral ledger by the company’s accounts department, once the buyers have confirmed that \nthe GRN, proof of delivery note and PO have been matched. \nReceipt and payment of invoices \nSuppliers send invoices directly to the accounts department for payment. Invoices and \npayments are processed by two clerks in Anglia’s accounts department. On receipt of the \ninvoice, the accounts department clerks will enter the invoice details into the general ledger \nrecognising a creditor for the amount specified on the invoice.  \nOn a weekly basis, a payment run is created by one of the accounts clerks which includes \ndetails of payment due dates for each invoice. This data is used to extract the payment run \ninformation each week. Invoices selected for payment in the weekly payment run are marked in \nthe creditors ledger as paid once the payment run has been processed. \nThe payment run is made by BACS based on a file automatically uploaded by the accounts \npayable clerk to the online banking system. The financial controller approves the payment in the \nonline banking system after detailed review the individual invoices supporting the payment run. \nMost suppliers send statements on a monthly basis to the accounts department but supplier \nqueries are only dealt with as they arise and statements are filed alphabetically each month. \n",
