    "id": "aa-ws2-slide-automated",
    "type": "WS",
    "module": "Module6",
    "source": "ICAS AA | Workshop 2 | Slide activity | Manual versus automated controls",
    "provider": "ICAS",
    "q": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-ws2-slide-automated-q.png\" alt=\"Workshop 2 original source extract\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "a": "<figure class=\"aa-source\"><img class=\"source-facsimile\" src=\"assets/aa-ws2-slide-automated-a.png\" alt=\"Workshop 2 original source extract\" loading=\"eager\"></figure>",
    "isHTML": true,
    "workshopOrigin": "slides",
    "searchText": "Manual vs automated controls\n.\nStudents often assume that automated controls are always better than manual controls. \nIn fact, a well-designed manual control with can be appropriate and, in some cases, better than an \nautomated control.\nConsider the two examples of automated controls below – what could go wrong in their operation?\nICAS AA Workshop 2\n78\nExample 1\n•\nAn audit client has several large customers in \nEurope to whom sales are made in Euros. \nThe associated receivables balances are \ntranslated into GDP at the year-end date, \nusing a rate which pulls automatically from a \nreputable third-party website. As this control \nis automatic, no review of the FX rate is \nperformed.\n•\nDuring the year, the third-party moved to a \nnew website domain.\nExample 2\n•\nAn audit client routinely posts an automatic \ndeferred revenue journal each month, based \non long-standing contract terms. As this \ncontrol is automatic, no review of these \njournals is performed.\n•\nDuring the year, there is a change to the \nterms of the customer contract and revenue \nshould now be recognised immediately.\n",
    "sourceFiles": [
      "sources/AA WS2 T2 2026 Solutions included.pdf"
    ],
    "linkTags": [
      "aa-module-6"
    ]
